  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 247 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nSaturday through Wednesday:\n\nVery warm temps are expected on Saturday due to persistent and\ndry southwest flow originating from the western states. This is\ndue to a broad upper trough over the Pacific Northwest and a \nresultant surface low over the Northern Plains, and high \npressure over the northeastern States. With 925mb temps of 20 to\n21C over southern WI and efficient mixing of a very dry air \nmass right up to that level (and likely a weak inversion above \nthat), we can expect the high temps to be right around 80 (up to\n82). The record high for that day is 84, so we can say that our\nhighs will approach the record. \n\nA tropical disturbance over the Gulf of America is expected to \nstrengthen and then track across the Ohio River Valley on Sunday.\nSoutheast WI will likely see cloud cover from this system (but\nno rain), which will keep temps in the lower- mid 70s instead \nof upper 70s).\n\nA northern stream mid level trough is expected to slide across\nUS/Canadian border and Lake Superior Monday or Tuesday (could be\na series of waves) and this will push a cold front down through\ncentral and eventually southern WI. Showers and perhaps a few\nthunderstorms will likely accompany this front, but the timing\nand rainfall amounts are still very uncertain. \n\nCooler, more seasonable temperatures will return around the\nmiddle of next week. \n\nCronce",
